+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded to collect a pantry order, or proposes sending the reminders by email to
+save the SMS cost. The day before pickup, larder texts each member that their pantry order is ready. Reminders go by
+SMS, not email, because when they went by email in 2025, missed pickups more than doubled and the fresh food in the
+uncollected bags was thrown away.
+
+## Sending
+
+`remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends one message the day before the pickup slot: "Your pantry order is ready for
+pickup", followed by the slot. `CHANNEL` picks the channel: `"sms"` texts the member's phone; any other value emails
+the member instead. The email path is still in the code, but no one should switch to it without reading the next
+section.
+
+## Why SMS, not email
+
+SMS reminders cost about 40 euros a month and email is free, so volunteers keep proposing email. Leo was the third
+person to ask in one year
+([issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md)). It has been tried:
+
+- From April to June 2025, reminders went by email. The email provider's report showed that members never opened 34%
+  of them. Missed pickups went from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is a packed bag, and the fresh
+  food in it goes in the bin by closing time.
+- Many members share one phone with their family and never check email, but they do see a text.
+- Reminders went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by August.
+
+Issue 52 closed with the maintainer's decision to keep SMS. A proposal to drop SMS needs to explain how it will
+keep missed pickups near 6% for members who don't read email.
